    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d566">

                <pb facs="1028" n="934" xml:id="pg934"/>

                <head>No. 566.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Buck.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-04-29"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 29,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 130.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your No. 212, of February
                    28, containing correspondence in regard to the action of the Peruvian Congress
                    “declaring null all acts of the Piérola and Iglesias Governments.”</p>
                <p>The views of the Government of the United States having been announced as to the
                    general principles involved in the assumptions of tha Peruvian legislation, and
                    exception in principle duly taken thereto, the matter may now rest, unless some
                    specific case should arise affecting American interests and calling for renewed
                    representations.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
